1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,422
Ahoj. This is Liam, in for Bear, with chapter two of INFO 7375, Computational

2
00:00:04,442 --> 00:00:08,863
Skepticism for AI, at Northeastern. This film is about a test that is ninety-nine

3
00:00:08,883 --> 00:00:12,988
percent accurate, and a positive result that means about one percent. Count ten

4
00:00:13,008 --> 00:00:17,112
thousand people, and the positives come mostly from the healthy: about a hundred

5
00:00:17,132 --> 00:00:21,554
false, one true. The trap: reading the test's accuracy as the answer, and forgetting

6
00:00:21,574 --> 00:00:23,140
how rare the disease was.

7
00:00:23,210 --> 00:00:26,826
Why should you care? Every alert you will ever triage, fraud, an intrusion, a

8
00:00:26,846 --> 00:00:30,721
screening flag, is this test. When the thing you hunt is rare, even an excellent

9
00:00:30,741 --> 00:00:34,357
detector fires mostly on the innocent. Miss that, and your team learns the alarms

10
00:00:34,377 --> 00:00:38,772
mean nothing, then ignores the real one. See the base rate, and you know what a flag

11
00:00:38,792 --> 00:00:40,590
is worth before you act on it.

12
00:00:40,660 --> 00:00:45,636
The real question: how likely is a positive to mean disease? Not how likely a sick

13
00:00:45,656 --> 00:00:50,319
person is to test positive. That is the ninety-nine percent on the box. Flip the

14
00:00:50,339 --> 00:00:51,880
question, and the answer changes.

15
00:00:51,950 --> 00:00:56,471
Three terms. Base rate: how common the condition is before any test. Here, one in

16
00:00:56,491 --> 00:01:00,407
ten thousand. False positive: a positive result on someone who is actually healthy.

17
00:01:00,427 --> 00:01:05,856
And the vertical bar, read 'given': P of A given B is the chance of A, counting only

18
00:01:05,876 --> 00:01:07,370
the cases where B happened.

19
00:01:07,440 --> 00:01:12,060
One patient. A disease that about one person in ten thousand has. A test the company

20
00:01:12,080 --> 00:01:15,830
says is ninety-nine percent accurate. The result comes back positive. So how likely

21
00:01:15,850 --> 00:01:20,760
is it that this patient is sick? The rail at the bottom is where the answer goes.

22
00:01:20,780 --> 00:01:22,790
For now it holds a question mark.

23
00:01:22,860 --> 00:01:26,929
First, what the ninety-nine percent promises. Take a hundred people who really are

24
00:01:26,949 --> 00:01:31,018
sick, one square each. Run the test. Ninety-nine light up positive. One slips

25
00:01:31,038 --> 00:01:35,736
through. That is the test's hit rate on the sick: ninety-nine out of a hundred,

26
00:01:35,756 --> 00:01:36,680
exactly as printed.

27
00:01:36,750 --> 00:01:40,802
Now a hundred healthy people. Ninety-nine percent accurate also means the test

28
00:01:40,822 --> 00:01:45,553
clears ninety-nine of them. One lights up anyway: a false positive. One in a

29
00:01:45,573 --> 00:01:49,965
hundred. Small. Remember that one. Real tests print two separate rates here; this

30
00:01:49,985 --> 00:01:52,340
example uses one number for both jobs.

31
00:01:52,410 --> 00:01:56,816
The obvious answer: the test is ninety-nine percent accurate, it said positive, so

32
00:01:56,836 --> 00:02:01,243
about ninety-nine percent. Most engineers say above ninety. In a classic 1978 study,

33
00:02:01,263 --> 00:02:05,329
most of sixty Harvard physicians and students answered about ninety-five, on a

34
00:02:05,349 --> 00:02:08,734
one-in-a-thousand version. But look at what the ninety-nine measured: positive,

35
00:02:08,754 --> 00:02:12,820
given sick. We were asked the other way round: sick, given positive.

36
00:02:12,890 --> 00:02:16,903
So stop looking at one patient. Picture everyone who takes the test: ten thousand

37
00:02:16,923 --> 00:02:21,223
people, one square each. On average, one of them is sick. There, one square. The

38
00:02:21,243 --> 00:02:24,968
other nine thousand nine hundred ninety-nine are healthy. Now both facts we just

39
00:02:24,988 --> 00:02:27,560
watched can act on the whole crowd at once.

40
00:02:27,630 --> 00:02:31,496
Before anything lights up: commit. The test is about to run on all ten thousand. The

41
00:02:31,516 --> 00:02:35,624
sick one will light. One in a hundred of the healthy will light too. Of all the

42
00:02:35,644 --> 00:02:39,510
squares that light, what share will be the sick one? Pick a number. Lock it in.

43
00:02:39,580 --> 00:02:43,897
Run it. The sick square lights. Now one percent of the healthy: about a hundred

44
00:02:43,917 --> 00:02:47,945
squares, scattered everywhere. Pull every lit square into the pen. One sick. About a

45
00:02:47,965 --> 00:02:52,571
hundred healthy. The positive you are holding is one of a hundred and one. About one

46
00:02:52,591 --> 00:02:52,860
percent.

47
00:02:52,930 --> 00:02:57,241
What we just did deserves its name. The chance of disease given a positive is the

48
00:02:57,261 --> 00:03:01,572
sick square's share of the pile. On top: how common the disease is, times how often

49
00:03:01,592 --> 00:03:05,091
the sick test positive. Underneath: the whole pile. That same piece, plus the

50
00:03:05,111 --> 00:03:09,152
healthy share times the false alarm rate. That is Bayes' theorem. And look where the

51
00:03:09,172 --> 00:03:12,400
base rate sits: on top, with the same weight as the test.

52
00:03:12,470 --> 00:03:16,625
Read the line. Left: the chance of disease, given a positive. On top: the base rate,

53
00:03:16,645 --> 00:03:20,278
one in ten thousand, times the hit rate, point nine nine. Underneath: that same

54
00:03:20,298 --> 00:03:24,192
piece, plus the healthy share times the false alarm rate, point zero one. Every way

55
00:03:24,212 --> 00:03:28,367
to get a positive, added up. Plug in the numbers: about point zero zero nine eight.

56
00:03:28,387 --> 00:03:32,803
The count said one in a hundred and one, because it let the test catch the sick

57
00:03:32,823 --> 00:03:34,890
person for certain. Both are about one percent.

58
00:03:34,960 --> 00:03:39,294
Back to our patient. Before the test: one in ten thousand. After a positive: about

59
00:03:39,314 --> 00:03:43,937
one in a hundred. The test did real work, almost a hundredfold. And the answer is

60
00:03:43,957 --> 00:03:48,291
still about one percent. Both are true. The same shape shows up at hospital scale:

61
00:03:48,311 --> 00:03:52,064
one widely used sepsis model, checked externally, alerted on eighteen percent of all

62
00:03:52,084 --> 00:03:56,418
patients, and still missed two thirds of the sepsis. Not found here: how common a

63
00:03:56,438 --> 00:03:59,320
disease must be before a positive is a coin flip.

64
00:03:59,390 --> 00:04:02,917
Let's recap with Claude. A ninety-nine percent accurate test, a one-in-ten-thousand

65
00:04:02,937 --> 00:04:06,787
disease, a positive result: about a one percent chance of disease. Ninety-nine

66
00:04:06,807 --> 00:04:10,980
percent is positive given sick; the question was sick given positive. Count ten

67
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:15,172
thousand people: one true positive, about a hundred false. Bayes writes the count

68
00:04:15,192 --> 00:04:20,010
down, and the base rate sits on top, with the same weight as the test.

69
00:04:20,080 --> 00:04:25,024
Your turn. Paste this into Claude: Take a test that catches 99% of sick people and

70
00:04:25,044 --> 00:04:29,057
clears 99% of healthy ones. Before computing anything, predict the base rate at

71
00:04:29,077 --> 00:04:33,711
which a positive result becomes a coin flip: a 50% chance of disease. Then compute

72
00:04:33,731 --> 00:04:39,605
the chance of disease given a positive at base rates of 1 in 100, 1 in 1,000, 1 in

73
00:04:39,625 --> 00:04:44,259
10,000 and 1 in 100,000 — first by counting a population, then with Bayes' theorem.

74
00:04:44,279 --> 00:04:49,223
Finally, name one detector in my own field and the base rate it works against. Two

75
00:04:49,243 --> 00:04:54,186
things to look for. Did it lock a prediction before it computed? If it jumped to

76
00:04:54,206 --> 00:04:58,840
numbers, send it back. And did the counting and Bayes agree at every base rate?

77
00:04:58,910 --> 00:05:04,107
The Test That Is Ninety-Nine Percent Accurate. At Nik Bear Brown.
